<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prefect</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as prefects</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A prefect is an official placed in charge of an administrative unit or service. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> prefects held major military and administrative posts, including the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> was organised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> administered by prefects after 1848, like metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prefect.jpg" alt="Prefect" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gzen92 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pr%C3%A9fet_Louis_Laugier_(Colmar).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> praefectus, from praeficere, meaning to put in front, that is, in charge. A prefect's office or area of control is a prefecture. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> praefectus was the formal title of many officials, from fairly low to high rank. Their authority was not embodied in their person, as it was with elected magistrates, but conferred by delegation from a higher authority, and they could control prisons and take part in civil administration. In medieval Latin the word named various administrative, military and judicial officers, often alongside a vernacular term such as Burggraf, literally count of the castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the function was created on 17 February 1800 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, after the coup of 9 November 1799 made him First Consul. The prefect was the national government's top representative and chief administrator in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, helped by subprefects in the arrondissements. Decentralising laws from 1982, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>, left the prefect as the State's chief representative but without the role of chief administrator. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> took over the model during the Napoleonic occupation from 1802, and its present form dates from 1861, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bettino-ricasoli">Bettino Ricasoli</a>'s government extended the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>'s system to the whole country. The Italian prefect enforced central authority in education, public works and public health, and in naming mayors and provincial deputies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> adopted the French model, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> a prefecture and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> formed two levels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> had elected prefects of 54 prefectures until the Kallikratis reform of 2010. In Albania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> prefects are government appointees, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> a prefect is the elected head of a municipality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> also uses the title, for the heads of curial congregations, often cardinals, and for a prefect apostolic, who leads an apostolic prefecture, a mission territory not yet a diocese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prefect” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Charlemagne deposed the last Agilolfing in 788 it was governed by Frankish <b>prefects</b> and then became the core of the East Frankish kingdom of Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For the periods of rule by Israel and Judah it describes governors or <b>prefects</b> placed over the Edomites by David, a form of government that seems to have continued under Solomon, and, in the time of Jehoshaphat (c. 870–849 BCE), a king of Edom who was probably a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Algeria was governed as an extension of France itself after 1848, with departments, <b>prefects</b>, and French civil law applying to European settlers while most indigenous Algerians remained under the indigénat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Administration was centralised on the French model, with <b>prefects</b> governing the provinces from Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Diocletian broke up the single province and brought Egypt into the empire's common system of taxation and coinage, and in the later fourth century, probably around 381, a separate diocese of Egypt was created under an Augustal <b>Prefect</b> resident in Alexandria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Equestrian <b>prefects</b> governed Egypt and commanded the Praetorian Guard, procurators ran the imperial estates and taxes, and equestrian officers commanded auxiliary units.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li></ul></section>
